[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Deque (Double-Ended Queue) in Data Structures & Algorithms"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Deque (pronounced "deck") stands for Double-Ended Queue. It is a versatile data structure that generalizes both stacks and queues. Unlike a regular queue, where elements can only be added at the rear and removed from the front, a deque allows insertion and deletion from both ends."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Characteristics of a Deque"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insertion at both ends: Elements can be added to the front (head) or the rear (tail) of the deque."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deletion from both ends: Elements can be removed from the front (head) or the rear (tail) of the deque."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: Deques can be used to implement both stacks (LIFO) and queues (FIFO) by restricting operations to one end."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Deque Operations"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"addFront(item): Adds an item to the front of the deque."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"addRear(item): Adds an item to the rear of the deque."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"removeFront(): Removes and returns the item from the front of the deque. Returns null or an error if the deque is empty."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"removeRear(): Removes and returns the item from the rear of the deque. Returns null or an error if the deque is empty."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"peekFront(): Returns the item at the front of the deque without removing it. Returns null if the deque is empty."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"peekRear(): Returns the item at the rear of the deque without removing it. Returns null if the deque is empty."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"isEmpty(): Checks if the deque is empty."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"size(): Returns the number of items in the deque."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation of a Deque"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Deques can be implemented using various data structures, including:"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arrays (fixed-size or dynamic arrays)"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Linked Lists (doubly linked lists are preferred for efficient insertion/deletion at both ends)"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's a simple example of a Deque implementation using a Python list:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"class Deque:
    def __init__(self):
        self.items = []

    def isEmpty(self):
        return len(self.items) == 0

    def addFront(self, item):
        self.items.insert(0, item)

    def addRear(self, item):
        self.items.append(item)

    def removeFront(self):
        if not self.isEmpty():
            return self.items.pop(0)
        else:
            return None

    def removeRear(self):
        if not self.isEmpty():
            return self.items.pop()
        else:
            return None

    def peekFront(self):
        if not self.isEmpty():
            return self.items[0]
        else:
            return None

    def peekRear(self):
        if not self.isEmpty():
            return self.items[-1]
        else:
            return None

    def size(self):
        return len(self.items)

# Example usage:
d = Deque()
d.addFront(1)
d.addRear(2)
print(d.removeFront()) # Output: 1
print(d.removeRear())  # Output: 2"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Applications of Deques"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Undo/Redo functionality in software applications."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Web browser history (back and forward buttons)."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implementing algorithms like A-star search."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Task scheduling algorithms."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Palindrome checker (checking if a string is the same forwards and backward)."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Time Complexity"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"With a good implementation (e.g., using a doubly linked list or a dynamic array with amortized analysis), most deque operations (addFront, addRear, removeFront, removeRear, peekFront, peekRear) have a time complexity of O(1)."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages of Using Deques"},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility in handling data from both ends."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Efficient implementation of both stacks and queues."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Disadvantages of Using Deques"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Slightly more complex to implement compared to simple queues or stacks."},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, a deque is a powerful and flexible data structure that provides efficient insertion and deletion from both ends, making it suitable for a wide range of applications."}]
